@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        The law was the grammar of theology, which, after carrying its scholars a short way, 
        handed them over to faith to be completed. Thus, Paul compares the Jews to children, and 
        us to advanced youth.
    </span>
    <span>John Calvin, Commentary on Galatians</span>
</div>
    
<ol>
    <li>
        The old covenant was received by Moses on Mount Sinai, and it contained provisions for the old law, 
        the temple sacrifices, circumcision, and other ceremonies which have since been
        fulfilled in Christ.
    </li>
    <li>
        The law of Moses was given to the Israelites as a partial fulfilment of the promise to make Abraham
        a great nation. However, the law was never meant to justify, but to instruct God's people in the ways of
        righteousness, to reveal sin so that it may be seen and defeated, and to foreshadow Christ's 
        once-for-all sacrifice on the cross.
    </li>
    <li>
        In this way the old covenant had an internal administration of those who held to the faith of Abraham, and an
        external administration of those who obeyed the rites and ceremonies. As Saint Paul teaches in the epistle
        to the Romans, not all Israel was true Israel, but only those who had faith.
    </li>
    <li>
        The sacraments of the old covenant were circumcision, which was the sign and seal of God's promise to Moses,
        and the passover, which commemorated God's deliverance of Israel out of Egypt. These sacraments were part of
        the old covenant's external administration but did not themselves impart grace to those who partook of
        them.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />